# Erdős Problem #99 — Target A Euclidean Steiner-tube sector separator

**Date:** 2026-09-05  
**Branch:** `research/target-c-c97-caterpillar-fan-capacity-2026-09-04`  
**Run type:** exact Schur separator theorem; population bookkeeping corrected  
**Target:** plateau-terminalized Target A response  
**Status:** LEVEL I STILL OPEN

## 0. Audit correction

The original version correctly proved the geometric Schur-linkage bound ST3, but its
subsequent population equivalence used `X_i=X^* cap D_i` after deleting tube sites from
`Z`.  That omits the fact that a deleted basis site `z in Z cap T_Gamma` has a matched
basis partner `mu_0^{-1}(z)` which is absent from the surviving matched-pair count.
The Schur inequality itself does not use that erroneous cancellation and remains valid.
The corrected population form below removes those matched basis partners explicitly.

## 1. A short three-leaf tree in every diameter-d convex body

Let `K=K^*` be the convex response hull, with `diam(K)=d`. Choose three distinct
generic points `p_1,p_2,p_3` on `partial K`. They can be chosen so that the Euclidean
Steiner tree `Gamma` joining them meets `X^*` nowhere and meets `partial K` only at its
three prescribed leaves. The excluded choices form a finite union of lower-dimensional
incidence conditions; small generic changes of the boundary points preserve the length
bound below.

Write the side lengths of the boundary triangle as `a,b,c`; all are at most `d`.

### Lemma ST1 — Steiner length bound

The Euclidean Steiner tree joining `p_1,p_2,p_3` has total length

`L(Gamma)<=sqrt(3)d`.

### Proof

If all triangle angles are below `120 degrees`, the standard Fermat/Steiner formula is

`L(Gamma)^2=(a^2+b^2+c^2+4sqrt(3)Area)/2`.

Since `a,b,c<=d` and a triangle with all sides at most `d` has area at most
`sqrt(3)d^2/4`,

`L(Gamma)^2<=3d^2`.

If one angle is at least `120 degrees`, the Steiner tree is the two sides adjacent to
that angle. Calling those sides `b,c`, the opposite side obeys

`d^2>=a^2>=b^2+c^2+bc`.

Since `bc<=(b+c)^2/4`,

`(b+c)^2<=d^2+(b+c)^2/4`,

so `b+c<=2d/sqrt(3)<sqrt(3)d`. QED.

The tree is a union of at most three straight segments and is embedded. In the
nondegenerate three-leaf case, `K\Gamma` has three outer components
`D_1,D_2,D_3`. The two-edge degeneracy can be handled by the same argument with the
actual complement components.

## 2. Unit-tube shielding for an arbitrary geometric tree

Let

`T_Gamma={y in Y_in : dist(y,Gamma)<1}`,

and split

`T_Z=T_Gamma cap Z`,

`T_O=T_Gamma cap O`.

### Lemma ST2 — every surviving response edge avoids Gamma

If `xy` is a response radial edge with `y notin T_Gamma`, then its segment does not
intersect `Gamma`.

### Proof

If the segment met `Gamma` at `z`, then `z` lies on `[x,y]` and

`dist(y,Gamma)<=|y-z|<=|y-x|<1`,

contradicting `y notin T_Gamma`. QED.

Because `Gamma cap X^*=emptyset`, every path in the induced response graph using no
right vertex of `T_Gamma` is trapped in one component `D_i`.

Fix the Section-113 `Z`-perfect basis matching `mu_0:B->Z` and put

`B_T=mu_0^{-1}(T_Z)`.

If `z notin T_Z` and `b=mu_0^{-1}(z)`, the matching segment `bz` cannot meet `Gamma`,
so `b,z` lie in the same sector. Restricting the matching therefore gives a bijection
between

`B_i=(B\B_T) cap D_i`

and

`Z_i=(Z\T_Z) cap D_i`.

Hence

`|B_i|=|Z_i|`.

No basis-closure enlargement is required; one only has to remove the matched partners
of the tube sites that were actually deleted.

## 3. Exact Schur sector bound

For each sector define the surviving terminal set

`C_i^partial=C^partial cap D_i`.

For a surviving exterior sink `o in O\T_O`, put it in `O_i` when it has a surviving
response neighbor in `D_i`.

A surviving right vertex cannot have neighbors in two distinct sectors. Indeed, if
`x_i,x_j` were such neighbors, the segment `x_i x_j` meets `Gamma`; since both lie in
the convex open disk `B(o,1)`, that intersection point also lies in `B(o,1)`, putting
`o` in `T_Gamma`. Thus the `O_i` are pairwise disjoint.

Take a maximum vertex-disjoint directed Schur linkage from `C^partial` to `O`. At most
`|T_Gamma|` paths meet a tube right vertex. Every remaining path is sector-local and
ends at a distinct sink in the corresponding disjoint `O_i`. Therefore:

### Theorem ST3 — Steiner-tube Schur bound

`rank S <= |T_Gamma| + sum_i min(|C_i^partial|,|O_i|)`.

Consequently

`zeta >= sum_i (|C_i^partial|-|O_i|)_+ - |T_Gamma|`.

This theorem is the original report's valid core and is unchanged by the audit.

## 4. Corrected population form

Put

`X_i^surv=(X^*\B_T) cap D_i`,

`C_i^circ=C^circ cap D_i`.

The geometric tree avoids `X^*`, so there are no left vertices on `Gamma`. Since
`B_T subset B`, the surviving left population decomposes as

`X_i^surv=B_i sqcup C_i^circ sqcup C_i^partial`.

Together with `|B_i|=|Z_i|`, this gives

### Proposition ST3P — corrected sector population identity

`|C_i^partial|=|X_i^surv|-|Z_i|-|C_i^circ|`.

Hence the valid population version of ST3 is

`zeta >= sum_i (|X_i^surv|-|Z_i|-|O_i|-|C_i^circ|)_+ - |T_Gamma|`.

The old formula with `X_i=X^* cap D_i` is retracted.

## 5. Explicit pointwise tube cardinality

Let one straight segment of `Gamma` have length `ell`. Every lattice site at distance
strictly below one from it lies in the closed unit stadium around that segment. The
stadium is convex, with

`area=pi+2ell`,

`perimeter=2pi+2ell`.

Oler's finite packing inequality for the unit triangular lattice, with
`a_0=sqrt(3)/2`, gives at most

`(pi+2ell)/a_0 + (2pi+2ell)/2 + 1`

lattice sites in the stadium. Hence

`N_ell <= (1+4/sqrt(3))ell + 2pi/sqrt(3)+pi+1`.

The Steiner tree has at most three segments. A union bound and Lemma ST1 therefore
give

### Corollary ST4 — universal O(d) three-sector shield

`|T_Gamma|`

`<= (1+4/sqrt(3))L(Gamma)`

`   +3(2pi/sqrt(3)+pi+1)`

`<= (4+sqrt(3))d + 3(2pi/sqrt(3)+pi+1)`.

Numerically the leading coefficient is `4+sqrt(3)=5.7320508075...`.

No claim of sharpness is made. The separate right-deficiency report improves the
relevant averaged penalty by cancelling exterior tube sinks exactly.

## 6. Relation to the right-deficiency theorem

For the right deficiency `h=|O|-rank S`, exterior tube sinks cancel. The companion
report `target_a_right_deficiency_geometric_tube_2026-09-05.md` therefore gives a
strictly cleaner endpoint:

`h >= Xi_Gamma-|T_Z|`.

That theorem is not affected by the population correction here because it never
expresses the terminal counts through `X_i` and `Z_i`.

Thus future work should normally use the right-deficiency form unless a left-nullity
population identity is specifically needed.